 ## Overview
-The Multi-Level Array Grid is a hierarchical acceleration structure for ray tracing that recursively subdivides the scene bounding box into a nested grid of nodes [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficient spatial traversal by replacing pointer-chasing trees with contiguous array-based grids, optimizing for memory locality and reduced ray-primitive intersection counts [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike flat grids, it adapts resolution based on object density, and unlike tree structures, it uses fixed-size grid arrays at each level, allowing for faster traversal via integer arithmetic [S1, S2, unofficial]. It builds on the shared principles of hierarchical decomposition and ray-grid traversal defined in [Multi-level Grid Structures](6354-multi-level-grid-structures.md).
+The Multi-Level Array Grid is a hierarchical acceleration structure for ray tracing that recursively subdivides the scene bounding box into a nested grid of nodes [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficient spatial traversal by replacing pointer-chasing trees with contiguous array-based grids, optimizing for memory locality and reduced ray-primitive intersection counts [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike flat grids, it adapts resolution based on object density, and unlike tree structures, it uses fixed-size grid arrays at each level, allowing for faster traversal via integer arithmetic [S1, S2, unofficial]. It builds on the shared principles of hierarchical decomposition and ray-grid traversal defined in [Multi-level Grid Structures](6354-multi-level-grid-structures.md) [G].
 
